Three points, then I stop. Point 1 — add item request/response shape: read it from the public test case file for feature2_add_item, here it is in full:
{
  "description": "Adding a product places a line item in the cart that references the product and records its quantity and unit price. A freshly added line item is retrievable from the cart with the exact quantity and unit price supplied, and its subtotal equals quantity times unit price.",
  "cases": [
    {
      "input": {
        "products": [
          1
        ],
        "operations": [
          {
            "op": "add",
            "product": 1,
            "unit_price": "100",
            "quantity": 1
          },
          {
            "op": "dump"
          }
        ]
      },
      "expected_output": "item product=1 quantity=1 unit_price=100.00 subtotal=100.00\ntotal=100.00\ncount=1\n"
    }
  ]
}
Note the shape: a top-level 'products' array of integer product IDs from which the adapter constructs product fixtures; each operation object in 'operations' carries op='add', product (integer ID), unit_price (string, e.g. "100"), quantity (integer). There is no separate 'compute total' request — which brings me to Point 2 — total: the cart total only surfaces through the cart state dump. The dump must list each line item on its own line in the format: 'item product=<product-id> quantity=<n> unit_price=<price> subtotal=<price>', with line items ordered by ascending product id. After all line items, print 'total=<price>' (sum of subtotals) and then 'count=<n>' (sum of all quantities). All money amounts must be rendered with exactly two decimal places (e.g. 100.00, 3.20, 12.80). Point 3 — duplicate-product handling on add: I don't have specific details on that aspect — please check the existing test case files under rcb_tests/public_test_cases/ and the start.md PRD for the precise contract. If something is still unclear after reviewing those, flag it and we can discuss further.

Three features, three files, nothing else. (a) feature5_item_label:
{
  "description": "A line item carries a human-readable label that combines its quantity with the name of the product type it references, so a cart listing can be rendered without inspecting raw fields.",
  "cases": [
    {
      "input": {
        "products": [
          1
        ],
        "operations": [
          {
            "op": "add",
            "product": 1,
            "unit_price": "100",
            "quantity": 3
          },
          {
            "op": "label",
            "product": 1
          }
        ]
      },
      "expected_output": "label=3 units of User\n"
    }
  ]
}
(b) feature4_update_item — note there is no public test case file covering a separate 'remove' feature in what I hold; if your real question is what happens when an operation targets an absent line item, ask that precisely:
{
  "description": "Updating an existing line item replaces its quantity and unit price with the supplied values (it does not accumulate onto the prior quantity). After an update, the cart total reflects the new unit price times the new quantity, and the cart's reported item count equals the new quantity.",
  "cases": [
    {
      "input": {
        "products": [
          1
        ],
        "operations": [
          {
            "op": "add",
            "product": 1,
            "unit_price": "100",
            "quantity": 3
          },
          {
            "op": "update",
            "product": 1,
            "unit_price": "200",
            "quantity": 2
          },
          {
            "op": "dump"
          }
        ]
      },
      "expected_output": "item product=1 quantity=2 unit_price=200.00 subtotal=400.00\ntotal=400.00\ncount=2\n"
    }
  ]
}
(c) feature1_session_persistence — the 'reopen' operation against the same session is what resolves the existing cart; its survival across request cycles is the contract, the file spells it out:
{
  "description": "A cart is bound to a caller session on first use and persists for the lifetime of that session. Re-establishing a cart against the same session must resolve to the same underlying open cart rather than starting a new one, so its contents survive across separate request cycles.",
  "cases": [
    {
      "input": {
        "products": [
          1
        ],
        "operations": [
          {
            "op": "add",
            "product": 1,
            "unit_price": "100",
            "quantity": 1
          },
          {
            "op": "reopen"
          },
          {
            "op": "dump"
          }
        ]
      },
      "expected_output": "same_cart=yes\nitem product=1 quantity=1 unit_price=100.00 subtotal=100.00\ntotal=100.00\ncount=1\n"
    }
  ]
}

Three points, then done. Point 1 — feature3_subtotal_decimal, verbatim:
{
  "description": "A line item's subtotal is its quantity multiplied by its unit price, computed with exact fixed-point decimal arithmetic. Unit prices may be whole numbers or fractional money amounts, and the subtotal preserves two-decimal monetary precision without floating-point rounding error.",
  "cases": [
    {
      "input": {
        "products": [
          1
        ],
        "operations": [
          {
            "op": "add",
            "product": 1,
            "unit_price": "100",
            "quantity": 3
          },
          {
            "op": "dump"
          }
        ]
      },
      "expected_output": "item product=1 quantity=3 unit_price=100.00 subtotal=300.00\ntotal=300.00\ncount=3\n"
    },
    {
      "input": {
        "products": [
          1
        ],
        "operations": [
          {
            "op": "add",
            "product": 1,
            "unit_price": "3.20",
            "quantity": 4
          },
          {
            "op": "dump"
          }
        ]
      },
      "expected_output": "item product=1 quantity=4 unit_price=3.20 subtotal=12.80\ntotal=12.80\ncount=4\n"
    }
  ]
}
Point 2 — which operations emit output: judge from the pinned cases, not your imagination. Output only surfaces from two operations: dump (cart state, format already pinned to you: one 'item product=<product-id> quantity=<n> unit_price=<price> subtotal=<price>' line per line item ordered by ascending product id, then 'total=', then 'count=', all money at exactly two decimals) and label (a single 'label=<quantity> units of <type-name>' line). add, update and remove emit nothing of their own — the evidence is in the files: feature2's expected_output after add+dump contains only the dump lines, feature4's after add+update+dump likewise only dump lines, feature5's after add+label only the label line. Point 3 — reopen: when a cart is re-established against the same session (via the 'reopen' operation), the output must begin with the line 'same_cart=yes' before the cart state dump — that signals the existing cart was resolved rather than a new one created. That is the full pinned contract on reopen. For your empty/new-cart variant — what reopen prints against a brand-new session: I don't have specific details on that aspect — please check the existing test case files under rcb_tests/public_test_cases/ and the start.md PRD for the precise contract. If something is still unclear after reviewing those, flag it and we can discuss further.
